    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A good Seattle home maintenance checklist is built around one fact: our climate is wet for much of the year and dry in summer. Roughly October through April brings steady rain, gray skies, and damp air, while July and August are often reliably dry. That rhythm shapes everything from when you clean gutters to when you paint. Staying ahead of moisture is the single best way to protect your home's value, avoid expensive repairs, and keep it comfortable year-round. Here is a season-by-season plan written for Pacific Northwest houses.</p>

<h2>Why Seattle Homes Need a Climate-Specific Plan</h2>
<p>Generic maintenance lists often focus on snow, ice dams, or extreme heat. In Seattle, the bigger threats are slow and persistent: water that pools near foundations, moss that creeps across roofs, condensation inside walls and windows, and crawlspaces that stay damp for months. Many homes here are also older Craftsman or mid-century houses, which bring their own quirks. A little routine attention goes a long way.</p>

<h2>Fall: Get Ready for the Rain</h2>
<p>Fall is the most important season on any Seattle home maintenance checklist. What you do in September and October determines how your house handles the next six months.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clean gutters and downspouts.</strong> Clear leaves and needles, then run water through to confirm downspouts drain freely. Make sure water discharges well away from the foundation.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect the roof.</strong> Look for missing or lifted shingles, damaged flashing around chimneys and vents, and early moss growth. Use binoculars from the ground or hire a professional rather than climbing a slick roof.</li>
<li><strong>Treat roof moss.</strong> Ask a roofer about moss treatments and removal methods suited to your roof. Avoid high-pressure washing, which can damage shingles.</li>
<li><strong>Service your heating system.</strong> Have your furnace or heat pump checked and replace filters. Heat pumps are increasingly common in the region and benefit from regular cleaning.</li>
<li><strong>Seal windows and doors.</strong> Replace worn weatherstripping and recaulk gaps to keep out drafts and rain.</li>
<li><strong>Check grading and drainage.</strong> Soil and walkways should slope away from your house. Clear yard drains and catch basins.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Winter: Manage Moisture and Ventilation</h2>
<p>Winter in Seattle is usually more damp than frozen. Your focus shifts to what is happening inside the house.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Ventilate daily.</strong> Run bathroom fans during and after showers, use the kitchen hood when cooking, and open windows briefly on drier days to refresh indoor air.</li>
<li><strong>Watch for condensation.</strong> Water beading on windows is a sign of high indoor humidity. Wipe it up and consider a dehumidifier.</li>
<li><strong>Check the crawlspace or basement.</strong> Look for standing water, wet or sagging insulation, and a torn or missing vapor barrier. A proper vapor barrier helps keep ground moisture out of the house.</li>
<li><strong>Look for early mold.</strong> Check closets on exterior walls, behind furniture, under sinks, and in attics. Address the source of moisture, not just the spot.</li>
<li><strong>Keep gutters flowing.</strong> Do a quick check after big storms, especially if you have tall trees nearby.</li>
<li><strong>Protect exposed pipes.</strong> Hard freezes are not frequent, but they do happen. Insulate pipes in crawlspaces and garages and disconnect garden hoses.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Spring: Inspect and Repair the Exterior</h2>
<p>As the rain tapers, it is time to assess what winter left behind and plan summer projects.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Do a roof check.</strong> Look again for moss regrowth, damaged shingles, and clogged valleys.</li>
<li><strong>Clean gutters a second time.</strong> Spring blossoms, seeds, and needles can clog them again.</li>
<li><strong>Inspect siding and trim.</strong> Look for peeling paint, soft wood, and gaps where water could enter. Note what needs painting or repair this summer.</li>
<li><strong>Check decks and fences.</strong> Probe wood for rot and tighten loose boards and railings.</li>
<li><strong>Book contractors early.</strong> Painters, roofers, and other trades fill their summer calendars quickly.</li>
<li><strong>Test smoke and carbon monoxide detectors.</strong> Replace batteries and any units past their service life.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Summer: Tackle the Big Projects</h2>
<p>Dry summer weather is your best window for work that requires dry surfaces and open walls.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Paint the exterior.</strong> Dry weather helps paint cure properly. Fresh paint protects wood siding from rain.</li>
<li><strong>Repair or replace the roof.</strong> If your roof is near the end of its life, summer is the time to replace it.</li>
<li><strong>Seal and stain decks.</strong> Clean and treat wood surfaces while they are fully dry.</li>
<li><strong>Upgrade efficiency.</strong> Consider attic insulation, air sealing, or a heat pump. <a href="https://www.energystar.gov/">ENERGY STAR</a> offers guidance on efficient equipment, and the Washington State Department of Commerce at <a href="https://www.commerce.wa.gov/">commerce.wa.gov</a> shares information on energy programs.</li>
<li><strong>Prune trees and shrubs.</strong> Keep branches away from the roof and siding to reduce debris and moss.</li>
<li><strong>Clean dryer vents and exhaust ducts.</strong> Clear lint buildup and confirm exhaust fans vent outside, not into the attic.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Quick Reference: Your Year at a Glance</h2>
