<tr><td>Winter</td><td>Ventilation, condensation, crawlspace moisture, mold checks</td></tr>
<tr><td>Spring</td><td>Exterior inspection, second gutter cleaning, book contractors</td></tr>
<tr><td>Summer</td><td>Exterior paint, roofing, decks, efficiency upgrades</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h2>Maintenance Pays Off When You Sell</h2>
<p>Buyers in Seattle often order thorough inspections, and inspectors know exactly where to look for PNW-specific problems like crawlspace moisture, roof moss, and failing gutters. A home with a clear maintenance history tends to inspect better and gives buyers confidence. If you are planning a move in the next year or two, keeping receipts and notes on completed work can help. Our <a href="/sell-your-home-seattle/">Seattle home selling guide</a> explains how to prepare your house for market, and if you are still shopping, our <a href="/buy-a-home-in-seattle/">buyer's guide</a> covers what to look for during inspections.</p>

